Venetium 0063 - Math.pow uses V8's own pow on Windows ARM32 (the ARM32 UCRT pow returns 0 for subnormal results).
Found by the device test run of the Thumb-2 V8 port (mjsunit es6/math-log2-log10 on a Surface RT): Math.pow(10, -310),
10 ** -310 and Math.pow(2, -1070) were 0 in every tier, --jitless included, while division, Math.sqrt and Math.exp
gave the subnormal results. V8's math::pow (v8/src/numbers/ieee754.cc) is behind every Math.pow and ** in all tiers and
behind the parser's and the compilers' constant folding. With v8_flags.use_std_math_pow (true everywhere but AIX) it
called std::pow = the statically linked UCRT pow, which on ARM32 installs its own FP control state (_ctrlfp) and returns
0 where the result is subnormal. On Windows ARM32 math::pow now always uses V8's own implementation
(base::ieee754::legacy::pow = LLVM libc pow, the AIX path); the flag is ignored there. Device-verified with the same
change in the Thumb-2 d8 on Windows RT 8.1: Math.pow(10, -310) = 1e-310 in every tier, and mjsunit math-pow,
es7/exponentiation-operator, es6/math-log2-log10 and the two regress tests that force --use-std-math-pow pass.
Other platforms are unchanged.

diff --git a/v8/src/numbers/ieee754.cc b/v8/src/numbers/ieee754.cc
index 1835062..e31ec6f 100644
--- a/v8/src/numbers/ieee754.cc
+++ b/v8/src/numbers/ieee754.cc
@@ -44,9 +44,14 @@ double pow(double x, double y) {
     }
   }
 
+#if defined(V8_OS_WIN) && defined(V8_TARGET_ARCH_ARM)
+  // Venetium: the Windows ARM32 UCRT pow returns 0 for subnormal results
+  // (Math.pow(10, -310)), so V8's own pow is used there.
+#else
   if (v8_flags.use_std_math_pow) {
     return std::pow(x, y);
   }
+#endif
   return base::ieee754::legacy::pow(x, y);
 }
 
